<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Italian War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Italian War of Independence was fought in 1848–1849 by the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> under Charles Albert, with volunteers from other Italian states, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>. Begun amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a>, it ended in Sardinian defeats at Custoza (1848) and Novara (1849), after which Charles Albert abdicated in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="First Italian War of Independence" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cesare Morbio (1812-1887) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Un_episodio_della_Battaglia_di_Novara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1848 revolutions broke out across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Ferdinand II of the Two Sicilies granted a constitution on 23 January, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Leopold II</a> of Tuscany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert of Sardinia</a> followed on 17 February, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> did so for the Papal States on 14 March. Sicily, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>. Riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, the Five Days, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> drove the Austrians from both cities, and from 23 March they fell back into the fortresses of the Quadrilateral: Peschiera, Verona, Mantua and Legnago. The Austrian army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Lombardy–Venetia</a> was commanded by the 81-year-old Josef Radetzky, whose soldiers were well trained and knew the ground.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 23 March 1848 the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and its army of about 65,000 men crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>. After fighting at Goito Bridge and Pastrengo, the Papal States and the Two Sicilies withdrew from the war, though some of their troops stayed on as volunteers. Radetzky took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> in June, and in the battle of Custoza from 22 to 27 July he defeated the Piedmontese. Charles Albert then sought a truce, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> surrendered, and the Salasco armistice followed in August.</p><p>Meanwhile a separate popular war was fought by revolutionaries. Pius IX fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 24 November 1848, after the assassination of his interior minister, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> commanded volunteers. Charles Albert reopened the war on 20 March 1849 under a Polish general, Wojciech Chrzanowski, but Radetzky invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and beat his army at Novara on 23 March. That night Charles Albert, finding his commanders against going on, declared that he would abdicate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The defeat at Novara echoed across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Leopold II returned to Tuscany, and Austrian troops imposed the restoration at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> on 10 and 11 May 1849. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> revolted on 1 April 1849, fearing that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Albertine Statute</a> would be abolished. Neapolitan forces under Carlo Filangieri took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a> on 7 April and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 15 May, ending the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Louis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sent an expedition to restore Pius IX in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> fell by 2 July. Venice, besieged by Julius Jacob von Haynau from 28 April, held out until 22 August 1849, when the war ended. Victor Emmanuel succeeded his father in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Italian War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goffredo Mameli, a young patriot from Genoa with republican and Jacobin sympathies, wrote the words in September 1847 (sources give the 8th or the 10th), inspired by the mass mobilisations that would lead to the revolutions of 1848 and the <b>First Italian War of Independence</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li></ul></section>
